  "body": {
    "id": "thm:trimmed-upper",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every \\(0<\\alpha\\le1/4\\), there exists an interval in \\(\\mathfrak C_\\alpha\\), measurable from \\(D\\), obtained by minimizing over \\(J\\), applying the observed-data block Horvitz--Thompson estimator on \\(J\\), and adding omitted contrast allowance \\(\\sum_{l\\notin J}w_l\\), whose length is at most \\(2\\min\\{1,\\rho_\\alpha(w,q)\\}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "alpha",
      "mathfrakC",
      "D",
      "B",
      "L",
      "l",
      "w",
      "rho",
      "q",
      "thetahat"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:honest-intervals",
      "def:trimmed-radius",
      "def:observed-block-means",
      "def:block-ht",
      "lem:observed-block-means",
      "lem:block-reduction",
      "thm:finite-upper"
    ],
    "justification": "Trimming prevents a low-weight, astronomically rare block from making the global interval inconsistent through inverse-probability amplification.",
    "gap": "KandirosHarshawSavje2026 removes difficult conflict-graph vertices for mean-squared error; this fixed-design procedure protects omitted causal contrast and retains finite-sample coverage.",
    "consumer": "The interval tells practitioners when to bias-protect a negligible rare block rather than report an explosive inverse-probability radius.",
    "proof_tex": "For a fixed subset \\(J\\), define the observed statistic \\(\\widehat\\theta_J=\\sum_{l\\in J}X_l(D)\\), the retained target \\(\\theta_J=\\sum_{l\\in J}w_l\\{\\mu_l(a)-\\mu_l(b)\\}\\), and \\(b_J=\\sum_{l\\notin J}w_l\\).  Outcome boundedness gives \\(|\\theta-\\theta_J|\\le b_J\\).  Repeating the proof of \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}finite\\text{-}upper}\\) on \\(J\\) gives, with probability at least \\(1-\\alpha\\),\n\\[\n |\\widehat\\theta_J-\\theta_J|\\le\n h_J:=\\sqrt{\\log(2/\\alpha)\\sum_{l\\in J}{w_l^2\\over q_l}}\n +{\\log(2/\\alpha)\\over3}\\max_{l\\in J}{w_l\\over q_l}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n I_J=[-1,1]\\cap[\\widehat\\theta_J-b_J-h_J,\\widehat\\theta_J+b_J+h_J]\n\\]\nis \\(D\\)-measurable, covers \\(\\theta\\) with probability at least \\(1-\\alpha\\), and has length at most \\(2\\min\\{1,b_J+h_J\\}\\).  There are finitely many subsets, so choose a deterministic minimizer \\(J^*\\), breaking ties lexicographically.  By \\(\\mathrm{def{:}trimmed\\text{-}radius}\\), \\(b_{J^*}+h_{J^*}=\\rho_\\alpha(w,q)\\).  Hence \\(I_{J^*}\\in\\mathfrak C_\\alpha\\) and has the claimed length.  For \\(J=\\varnothing\\), the statistic and maximum are zero, exactly as stipulated in the definition.",
